 We can find $m+n$ without writing the whole equation as a function of $y$. The axis of symmetry of this parabola is a vertical line that passes through the vertex (since the parabola does not have any tilt), and it divides the parabola into two symmetric halves.   We can find the axis of symmetry by completing the square, as follows: $$ \begin{aligned} 3y &= 2 \left( x^2 - 8x \right) + 18 \\ & = 2 \left( x^2 - 8x + 16 - 16 \right) + 18 \\ & = 2 \left( (x - 4)^2 - 16 \right) + 18 \\ & = 2 (x - 4)^2 - 14. \end{aligned} $$ Since the $x^2$ term has twice the coefficient of $x$ (in the linear term), the parabola's axis of symmetry is the line $x = 4$.   And since the $-14$ does not depend on $x$, $m = 4$ for all points on the parabola.   And since $y$ must equal $2 (x-4)^2 - 14$ when $x = 4$, no points on the parabola have $x$-coordinate $4.$   From the graph below, we see that none of the $y$-intermediate values are negative, because this parabola has the same or larger $y$-coordinate as the $y$-coordinate of any point on the parabola above or below it.   Therefore, the minimum value of $y$ for which $2x^2 - 16x + 18$ is real is $-14,$ and this minimum $y$-coordinate occurs when $x = 4$ else the fraction $\frac{2x^2 - 16x + 18}{3}$ would equal $-\infty$ when $x = 4.$  Therefore, $(m,4)$ is the minimum possible point on the parabola, with $y$-coordinate of $-14,$ hence $m = 4$ and $n = -14.$  And we know what the problem asks, so that means $m + n = \boxed{-10}.$

